A||N||20||dodger||86 2.8 running funny||||5||
Z||000000||dodger||01-23-2000||06:26 PM||||My 86 SE 2.8 is VERY clean with 90,000 mi. and has no other than the following problem:<P><BR>Here are the symptoms:<BR>Bucks when in first gear in parking lots. <P>Have to slip the clutch when starting from a stop or it dies.<P>Won't tach down and up smoothly when shifting between gears. So doesn't shift smoothly. Have to use too much clutch.<P>Idles high; 1.500 cold, 1000 warm. Idle varies about .o25 at times without touching accelerator.<P>(Something is cycling on and off at about 30 second intervals in the engine compartment. I can hear a metallic sounding switch turning on in the engine compartment followed by a whirr as if something with bearings is running. Then off again. I think there is a small loss of power when it is on.)<P>I took it TWICE to the local Chev dealership where the service manager said he was formerly at Pontiac. They did a standard tune-up for lots of money and did not address the problem at all. I'm afraid they will simply guess at the problem and keep replacing parts till they get lucky and fix it. Meanwhile I'm wearing out the clutch and using lots of gas at stoplights.<BR>   <BR>Thanks for any ideas.<BR>Dodger, near San Francisco. <P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||01-23-2000||06:40 PM||||The idle speeds are just about dead on. 1500 is normal cold, 900-1000 warm. Idle variation is also normal. The cycling sound sound, sounds like the a/c compressor kicking on and off. Loss of power also makes sense, the compressor eats power. For the bucking in parking lots, check your dogbone.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||dodger||01-23-2000||07:04 PM||||Thanks for the ideas Fiero56. <P>It seems like it is not getting gas at the right times. Driveability just isn't right. <P>Does the compressor cycle on and off all the time?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gokart||01-23-2000||07:14 PM||||Was any kind of distributor work done? Mine was running like yours a while back and the dumb mechanic never set the timing right. Check your timing!||||reg||1
Z||000004||dodger||01-23-2000||07:23 PM||||Thanks for the reply Gokart. I don't know when the timing was checked. I've only had it for 3 months.<P>I guess what I need is a knowledgeable mechanic I can trust.||||reg||3
Z||000005||fiero56||01-23-2000||08:07 PM||||The a/c compressor isn't supposed to cycle unless it is on. Maybe a switch or some other part that controls it is going haywire.<P>My v-6 Fiero hesitates and bucks when I try to drive it before the temp rises and the tach drops. I still haven't located the problem. It sounds like yours does it all the time though. They might be related though.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wrench||01-23-2000||08:41 PM||||Check you fuel pump. It may be failing. Have a gauge put on the schrader valve and see what you have, should be 45+ psi. Beyond that be sure your plugs, wires, dist cap and rotor are in excellent conditon. I would also get rid of the stock coil and put an MSD #8226 coil on it. These cars eat the ignition components and I have learned over 140K of ownership not to expect much beyond 20k out of any of them. Timing should be 10 degrees. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||cobra427||01-23-2000||10:37 PM||||If you are using either the heater control or the defroster control, your a/c will cycle on and off. I have read before why GM did this but the reason escapes me. It dose not cycle in the vent mode tho.||||reg||1
Z||000008||dodger||01-23-2000||11:27 PM||||Thanks Wrench, Thanks Cobra427,<BR>you guys are a wealth if information.<BR>I'll check to see if it is the A/C when the heater or defrost is on. It's very noticable. I can hear the switch and motor running from the driver's seat.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||01-23-2000||11:40 PM||||I didn't think the heater position turned on the compresor.  The only times the compressor turns on are for: max a/c, norm a/c, bi-lev a/c, and the defrost function.||||reg||1
Z||000010||cobra427||01-24-2000||12:34 AM||||fiero56, my 86GT does and a SE6000 w/2.8 that I had did the same thing. Check to see if yours doesn't as well. It was designed in to the system. Don't quote me on this but I have a small recolection that the reason being was ,in the cooler or colder months, it was a way to keep the system from stagnating(making the fluid cycle in the a/c system).There may have been other reasons, but that sticks out in my head.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||01-24-2000||12:53 AM||||Last I heard, you wanted to run the AC with the defrost is because the AC de-humidifies the air.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Phaeton||01-24-2000||02:12 AM||||The defrost switch cycles the ac compressor to keep the seals pliable, but it is not programmed to keep going off and on every thirty seconds, if it is then it needs a severe reprimand.  Bucking at low speed and light throttle? throttle position sensor? any trouble codes? good compression all around?  A bowl of chicken soup for you and a shot of everclear for the Fiero?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||cobra427||01-24-2000||02:16 AM||||standard, what about vehicles that don't have a/c? Are their defroster systems inferior? I thought that it was the warm air that was blowing on the w/shield that did the trick. I could be wrong,its happened before.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||fiero56||01-24-2000||08:17 AM||||I just dug up my '88 owners manual:<BR>For Models with a/c:<BR>...Heater: "Outside air is directed through the heater outlet and side window defoggers, with a small amount to the windshield. Another economy position."<P>Every other a/c controlled position(even defrost) mentions use of the a/c compressor. <BR>...Defrost: "When the outside air temperature is above 4C, the compressor will operate to provide outside conditioned(dry) air to the windshield, with ta small amount from the heater outlet and side window defoggers. Used in fogging and icing situations."<P>When I use the heater position(and I have a/c) the compressor doesn't do its normal "click" like the other positions. It is possible they changed the setup one year. Mine is an '88 and yours an '86 so its possible.<P>For vehicles without a/c:<BR>Defrost: "the maximum air flow to the windshield and side window defoggers with some air flow from the heater outlet."<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||WGraffiKs||01-24-2000||04:11 PM||||Dodger,<BR>This should be relatively to diagnose.  If you are sure the noise is from the engine compartment (rear) and not from the front, where the fan would give you the same symptoms.  Your symptoms sound to me more like the fan running, though. You could easily open both hood and trunk and watch as it idles.  If the fan is cycling on, you can see it turn.  If the A/C compressor cycles, you should see the center (or is the outer ring?) turn as the compressor clutch engages.  Don't stick fingers where they don't belong with the engine running!  If it's the A/C clutch, simply track down the signal source.  A manual and a voltmeter meter is all you'll need.<p>[This message has been edited by WGraffiKs (edited 01-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||stsmithgt||01-24-2000||07:25 PM||||Ahh Sooo, the A/C circulates itself with the heater functions, that means I don't have to turn the A/C on directly to get the same effect.  That will save on the ice build up on my ba!!s in the winter time that's for sure.  I wish I heard of this earlier. @@)||||reg||3
Z||000017||Phil||01-24-2000||10:53 PM||||If it is in fact your A/C compressor cycling that is usually an indication that your are low on freon.||||reg||1
Z||000018||stimpy||01-24-2000||11:04 PM||||Dodger, before you donate any more money to the Mr. Goodwrench pension fund, check out George at the FieroShop in Hayward. He is also a member of this fine forum. (fieroshop) Very knowledgeable guy and even offers towing services. Don't know if he will take it across the bridge, but I think he is worth the trip. If I still lived in Northern CA I wouldn't let anyone else touch my car.  (unsolicited endorsement) (George, if you would like to send a GT trunklid weatherstripping as compensation for my endorsement my dad can get you the address...86 GT on the flatbed at the July poker run)||||reg||1
Z||000019||dodger||01-25-2000||12:26 AM||||Thanks for all the info everyone. My Fiero is really cool and worth the effort of finding out all about it. Stimpy, I was thinking of calling the Fiero shop in Hayward. I saw the website. I really want to take it to a Fiero expert. <P>So...I figured out that whatever is cycling is activated by the defroster switch only. The A/C causes a greater power drain. The fan activates properly. I'll check the freon.<P>Also...TONIGHT when I started up, it idled at 3000 and went down to 2000 after a little while. So whatever is happening may be getting worse.<P>THEN...when I got home ACE my Walker Hound was limping around. Oh my...guess I'll try taking him to George at Fiero Shop. <p>[This message has been edited by dodger (edited 01-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||fiero56||01-25-2000||10:03 AM||||If your getting that high an idle check the one(2?) lines that run to the back of the throttle body and make sure they arent cracked/broken. Also check EGR tube for cracks, it will also give a high idle.||||reg||1
